    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p><strong>Plantar fasciitis is an overload problem in the plantar fascia, and the treatments with trial evidence are stretching, progressive heel-raise strength work, and a night splint if pain is worst on the first step.</strong> In 50 surgical specimens researchers found degeneration and no inflammation, which is why loading the tissue beats resting it.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Stretch first:</strong> plantar fascia-specific and calf stretching are recommended for both short- and long-term pain reduction in the 2023 clinical practice guideline.</li>
        <li><strong>Then load it:</strong> in 48 patients, high-load heel raises every second day beat daily stretching alone on foot function at 3 months.</li>
        <li><strong>Time matters:</strong> around 80% of people improve within a year, so judge any treatment against a condition that was going to settle anyway.</li>
        <li><strong>Honest limitation:</strong> no randomised trial has ever tested an oral collagen supplement for plantar fasciitis, including ours.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

