      <h2 id="inflammation">Is plantar fasciitis actually inflammation?</h2>
      <p>Mostly no, and the name is misleading. When Lemont and colleagues examined 50 surgical specimens of plantar fascia and published the results in the <em>Journal of the American Podiatric Medical Association</em> in 2003, they found myxoid degeneration, fragmentation of the fascia and vascular changes in the bone marrow — and failed to find histological evidence of inflammation. They argued the condition should be called a degenerative fasciosis.</p>
      <p>A 2024 review of the condition reached the same summary: the histology in plantar fasciitis shows chronic degenerative change rather than inflammation. One honest caveat is that specimens come from people who went to surgery, which is a small and unusually severe subgroup, so this is not the whole picture of everyone with a sore heel.</p>
      <p>It still changes what you should expect from treatment. Ice, rest and anti-inflammatory tablets can make a painful heel more comfortable, but if the tissue problem is degeneration under load, comfort is not the same as repair. That is the reasoning behind loading the tissue rather than only resting it — the same logic that underpins <a href="/pages/exercises-for-knee-pain">exercise programmes for knee pain</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="what-works">What actually works for plantar fasciitis?</h2>
      <p>Four things carry guideline-level support: stretching, strengthening, a night splint for morning pain, and insoles as an addition rather than a solo treatment. The 2023 heel pain clinical practice guideline in the <em>Journal of Orthopaedic &amp; Sports Physical Therapy</em> is the clearest statement of this, and it is worth noting what the same guideline tells clinicians <em>not</em> to do: do not use ultrasound to enhance stretching, and do not rely on electrotherapy where manual therapy, stretching and orthoses are available.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Plantar fascia-specific stretching.</strong> Sitting, cross the affected leg over the other knee, pull the toes back towards the shin and hold. DiGiovanni and colleagues randomised chronic heel pain patients to this versus a standard calf stretch; the plantar fascia-specific group did better at eight weeks. The two-year follow-up in the <em>Journal of Bone and Joint Surgery</em> in 2006, with 66 of the original 82 patients returning data, found both groups much improved and no significant difference between them on worst pain or first-step pain.</li>
        <li><strong>Calf stretching.</strong> The 2023 guideline recommends gastrocnemius and soleus stretching alongside the fascia-specific stretch, for short- and long-term pain reduction. A tight calf increases the tension the fascia has to absorb.</li>
        <li><strong>High-load strength training.</strong> Rathleff and colleagues randomised 48 patients with ultrasound-verified plantar fasciitis in <em>Scandinavian Journal of Medicine &amp; Science in Sports</em> in 2015. Both groups wore shoe inserts. One stretched daily; the other did progressive single-leg heel raises with a towel bunched under the toes, every second day. The strength group had better self-reported foot function at three months. Be clear-eyed about the limits: it was 48 people, the secondary outcomes showed no differences, and the twelve-month confidence intervals overlapped.</li>
        <li><strong>A night splint, if mornings are the problem.</strong> The guideline recommends a one- to three-month night splint programme specifically for people whose pain is consistently worst with the first step of the day.</li>
        <li><strong>Insoles, as an add-on.</strong> A meta-analysis of 19 studies found foot orthoses reduced pain versus sham at seven to twelve weeks without a consistent long-term benefit. The guideline advises against using them alone for short-term relief, and for using them with other treatments.</li>
      </ol>

