      <h2 id="how-long">How long does plantar fasciitis take to settle?</h2>
      <p>Months, usually, and most cases settle. A 2019 review in <em>American Family Physician</em> describes plantar fasciitis as typically self-limited, with around 80% of patients improving within one year. A 2025 literature review puts good results at 90% with six to twelve months of non-surgical treatment.</p>
      <p>That number cuts two ways. It is reassuring if you are three weeks in and frightened. It is also the reason to be sceptical of any product with a testimonial instead of a control group: an older abstract makes the point that the natural history of plantar fasciitis is poorly understood, which makes spontaneous recovery hard to separate from a response to treatment.</p>

