      <h2 id="india">Why heel pain is so common in India</h2>
      <p>Standing hours and body weight are the two risk factors that keep appearing, and both are common here. A cross-sectional study of 395 healthy street vendors in Delhi found heel pain in 46.1% of them, with higher BMI (odds ratio 1.15, 95% CI 1.08 to 1.21) and more hours standing per day (odds ratio 1.19, 95% CI 1.08 to 1.30) both associated with it. That sample is a standing occupation, so it overstates the rate for the general adult population, but it identifies the mechanism cleanly.</p>
      <p>Weight is the most consistent association in the wider literature. A systematic review of 25 papers in <em>Obesity Reviews</em> in 2012 concluded that increased BMI is strongly associated with chronic plantar heel pain in non-athletic people — while also finding only limited evidence that losing weight reduces foot pain. A 2023 Cureus study of plantar heel pain in a South Indian tertiary centre reported a mean BMI of 30.1 in its patients.</p>
      <p>Vitamin D comes up constantly in Indian clinics, and the evidence is weaker than the confidence with which it is quoted. A South Gujarat cross-sectional study of 108 adults reported a negative correlation between vitamin D and heel pain, and the South Indian study found about a quarter of its patients below 20 ng/ml — but these are cross-sectional associations, and no trial has shown that correcting vitamin D relieves heel pain. If you want the wider picture on that, we have written about <a href="/pages/vitamin-d-deficiency-symptoms">what vitamin D deficiency symptoms really are</a>.</p>

