      <h2 id="collagen">Does collagen help plantar fasciitis?</h2>
      <p>There is no evidence either way, and we would rather say so than imply otherwise. Searching the literature for an oral collagen supplement tested against plantar fasciitis returns no completed randomised trial. The closest thing is a 12-week double-blind placebo-controlled trial of hydrolysed collagen added to heavy slow resistance training in 64 athletes with tendinopathy, which stratified participants by site including plantar fascia pain — it is listed as active, not recruiting, and no results have been published.</p>
      <p>The studies people cite instead are about tendon and ligament, not fascia. Shaw and colleagues, in the <em>American Journal of Clinical Nutrition</em> in 2017, gave 5g or 15g of vitamin C-enriched gelatin an hour before six minutes of rope skipping and measured a collagen synthesis marker in blood applied to engineered ligament in the laboratory. Praet and colleagues reported in 2019 that collagen peptides added to an eccentric loading programme improved Achilles tendinopathy outcomes over six months. Both are interesting; neither measured a plantar fascia, and the first measured a laboratory marker rather than a healed tissue. We go through this evidence in more detail in our article on <a href="/pages/collagen-for-men">collagen for joints, tendons and recovery</a>.</p>
      <p>Two pieces of arithmetic about our own product belong here. <a href="/products/skelecoll">Skelecoll</a> supplies 10,000mg of hydrolysed marine collagen, which is a generous dose by the standards of what Indian labels usually contain, but it is generic hydrolysed marine collagen rather than the specific branded peptides used in the tendon trials above. And it contains 40mg of vitamin C from amla — enough to matter nutritionally, but a small number next to the vitamin C-enriched gelatin protocols people quote. If a supplement has a role in connective tissue, it is as raw material alongside loading, which is exactly the order the research tested it in. It is not a treatment for plantar fasciitis and should not be bought as one.</p>

