      <h2 id="doctor">When heel pain needs a doctor</h2>
      <p>Most heel pain is mechanical and settles with the programme above. Some of it is not plantar fasciitis at all, and a few patterns should be examined rather than stretched.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Both heels, especially under 45,</strong> with morning back stiffness, other painful joints, or eye or skin involvement. Bilateral heel enthesitis is a recognised presentation of inflammatory spondyloarthritis and needs blood tests rather than insoles.</li>
        <li><strong>Burning, tingling, shooting pain or numbness</strong> spreading into the heel and sole, which points towards nerve compression behind the inner ankle rather than the fascia.</li>
        <li><strong>Pain across the whole heel</strong> that is tender when the heel bone is squeezed from both sides, and worsens steadily with activity — a pattern consistent with a calcaneal stress fracture.</li>
        <li><strong>Pain at night or at rest,</strong> fever, or unexplained weight loss. Non-mechanical pain is a reason to be examined.</li>
        <li><strong>No improvement after six weeks</strong> of honest stretching and strengthening, or a sudden pop followed by severe pain and bruising.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>On injections: a corticosteroid injection has weak evidence for short-term relief, and retrospective series have reported plantar fascia rupture in roughly 2.4% of injected patients, with a higher figure in one other series. Fat pad atrophy is also reported, and adverse events have been inconsistently recorded across trials. That is a conversation to have with a surgeon, not a default first step.</p>

