      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>What is the fastest way to relieve plantar fasciitis?</summary>
          <p>There is no fast fix with trial evidence behind it. The quickest reliable relief in the guideline is stretching the plantar fascia and calf, plus a night splint if your pain is worst on the first step of the day. Expect improvement over weeks, not days, and remember that about 80% of people improve within a year regardless.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Should I rest or exercise with plantar fasciitis?</summary>
          <p>Neither complete rest nor pushing through it. The tissue problem is degenerative rather than inflammatory, so controlled loading is what the trials tested. In the 48-patient Rathleff trial, progressive single-leg heel raises every second day produced better foot function at three months than daily stretching alone.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does collagen help plantar fasciitis?</summary>
          <p>No randomised trial has tested an oral collagen supplement for plantar fasciitis. A 12-week trial in 64 athletes with tendinopathy, which included plantar fascia pain, is listed as active with no results published. The collagen studies people cite were done on tendon and ligament, not on the plantar fascia.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do insoles or orthotics work for plantar fasciitis?</summary>
          <p>They help in the short term as part of a programme. A meta-analysis of 19 studies found foot orthoses reduced pain versus sham at seven to twelve weeks without a consistent long-term benefit. The 2023 guideline advises against using them as a standalone treatment and supports using them alongside stretching and strengthening.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is plantar fasciitis caused by a heel spur?</summary>
          <p>Usually not. Heel spurs are common findings that are often painless, and the pain in plantar fasciitis comes from degenerative change in the fascia rather than from the bony spur itself. A 2023 study of painful heel spurs found obesity, with an odds ratio of about 2.7, to be the factor associated with pain.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Will losing weight fix my heel pain?</summary>
          <p>Higher BMI is the most consistent risk factor, but the evidence that losing weight resolves the pain is thinner than you would expect. The 2012 Obesity Reviews systematic review found a strong association between BMI and chronic plantar heel pain, and only limited evidence supporting weight loss as a treatment for foot pain.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can low vitamin D cause heel pain?</summary>
          <p>There are cross-sectional Indian studies linking lower vitamin D to heel pain, including one of 108 adults in South Gujarat, but no trial has shown that correcting vitamin D relieves it. Deficiency is common enough in India to be worth testing on its own merits rather than as a heel pain treatment.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

